ing  factors as likely to call for a sentence  more severe than the norm  two the killing of a child or a very old  or otherwise vulnerable victim  three evidence of sadism gratuitous  violence or sexual maltreatment  humiliation or degradation before The  Killing nine  the use of firearms or other dangerous  weapons whether carried for defensive or  offensive reasons  whilst a recommendation of a punitive  term longer than say 30 years will be  very rare indeed I do not think one  should set any upper limit  some crimes will certainly call for  terms very well in excess of the norm  and build an underlining and quote  brackets emphasis added  it will be seen therefore that the  letter advocated 14 years  as the quote's normal penalty for the  average normal or unexceptional murder  before taking aggravating and mitigating  factors into account  I should make clear that the emphasis  added above at paragraphs 2 3 and 9 is  agreed between the prosecution and the  defense as factors listed by Lord  Bingham which may apply in this case  I confirm I am satisfied that they do  apply in this case  I address Within These remarks the  mitigating features that apply I mean no  doubt whatsoever that you intended to  kill Jackie when you inflicted the Fatal  stab wounds  further guidance was issued in July 2000  which also referred to 14 years as a  starting point for adult murderers at  practice Direction  juveniles  murdered tariff  2001 weekly reports sorry 2001 one  weekly law reports one six five five  on the 31st of May 2002 the Lord chief  justice Henley down a practiced  direction for fixing the term for  offensive murder committed after that  date a practice statement life sentences  2002 to criminal appeal reports two  eight seven subsequently incorporated as  part of the Consolidated practice  Direction  the murder here was committed before the  31st of May 2002 and therefore that  practice direction does not apply  however it's clear that the practice  Direction wa